<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cerdanya</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cerdagne</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cerdanya is a high Pyrenean valley of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segre">Segre</a>, divided between Catalonia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Pyrénées-Orientales. It takes its name from the ancient Cerretani, became a Carolingian county and later passed to the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-the-pyrenees">Treaty of the Pyrenees</a> of 1659 split it, giving the northern part to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> but leaving the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llivia">Llívia</a> as a Spanish exclave.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cerdanya.jpg" alt="Cerdanya" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MarkCat · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ll%C3%ADvia_flors.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Cerdanya, also called La Cerdanya, is a natural comarca and historical region of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, historically one of the counties of Catalonia. Its land area is 1,086.07 km2, divided almost evenly between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> (50.3 percent) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> (49.7 percent). In 2001 it had about 26,500 inhabitants, of whom 53 percent lived on the Spanish side. Its only urban area is the cross-border Puigcerdà–Bourg-Madame, with 10,900 inhabitants in 2001. The area has around 3,000 hours of sunshine a year, and large solar installations have been built in French Cerdagne, among them the Mont-Louis Solar Furnace.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first inhabitants probably spoke a language related to old Basque and Aquitanian, as many place names show. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberians">Iberians</a> arrived from the south in the first millennium BCE and probably mixed with the natives, forming the Kerretes, whose name comes from a word for rock. Their main oppidum, Kere, stood above the modern village of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llivia">Llívia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> renamed it Julia Lybica. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> the area was the pagus Liviensis, part of Hispania Tarraconensis, and the name Cerdanya derives from Cerretania. The Kerretes seem to have kept their language until perhaps the 8th or 9th century. The diocese of Urgell was founded in the 6th century, with Cerdanya within its limits.</p><p>Devastated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> and other Germanic tribes, Cerdanya belonged to the Visigothic kingdoms and was later conquered by the Muslims. After the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> in 721 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> commander Uthman ibn Naissa set up a small realm there and allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odo-the-great">Odo the Great</a>. Under Carolingian pressure Cerdanya became a Frankish vassal about 785. The county arose from the Spanish Marches of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>. Wilfred the Hairy, count from 870 to 897, established his youngest son Miron as Count of Cerdanya. The county later gained Capcir and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conflent">Conflent</a>, and its counts were patrons of abbeys such as Saint-Michel de Cuxa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sant-marti-del-canigo">Saint-Martin-du-Canigou</a>, dedicated in 1009. The line died out in 1117 and the county passed to the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-the-pyrenees">Treaty of the Pyrenees</a> of 1659 split Cerdanya, the north becoming French and the south remaining Spanish; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llivia">Llívia</a> stayed Spanish, allegedly because the treaty ceded only villages and it counted as a city. Puigcerdà replaced Hix as capital in 1178. The Spanish side is the Catalan comarca of Baixa Cerdanya, with Puigcerdà as capital, while the French side lies in the département of Pyrénées-Orientales and has no special status. Its main towns are Bourg-Madame and the ski resort Font-Romeu. Tourism, skiing and the Yellow Train support the economy, and ties between families on both sides of the border remain close.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cerdanya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the late ninth century Wifred the Hairy, count of Barcelona, Girona, Urgell and <b>Cerdanya</b>, made his counties hereditary in his family and founded the monastery of Ripoll, and his successors in Barcelona gradually gained precedence over the other counts.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-catalonia">Principality of Catalonia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Cerretani occupied the high Pyrenean valley of <b>Cerdanya</b> and the upper Segre, today divided between Spain, France and Andorra.</blockquote><a href="/state/cerretani">Cerretani</a></li><li><blockquote>For much of the 10th century it was held together with <b>Cerdanya</b> by Wilfred's descendants, before acquiring its own line of counts.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-besalu">County of Besalú</a></li><li><blockquote>The high valley of <b>Cerdanya</b> came under Frankish rule around 798 and was organised as a county of the Marca Hispanica, often held with neighbouring Urgell.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-cerdanya">County of Cerdanya</a></li><li><blockquote>Urgell, in the upper Segre valley, was taken by the Franks around 798 and organised as a county of the Marca Hispanica, often joined with <b>Cerdanya</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-urgell">County of Urgell</a></li><li><blockquote>On his death in 1276 he left Majorca, Roussillon, <b>Cerdanya</b> and Montpellier to his younger son James II, creating an independent dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-majorca">Kingdom of Majorca</a></li></ul></section>
